par('[NOTA PARA EL AUTOR — ELIMINAR ANTES DE ENVIAR]\nV5. Todas las cifras de resultados proceden de la ejecución real en GPU (5 semillas, protocolo sin fugas) y son trazables a SMART_GRIDS_CODE_V4/results/: summary_v4.json, ablation_v4.json y Table*.csv. No queda ningún valor pendiente. Las once figuras se generan con figures_diagrams.py (Figs. 1-2) y figures_results.py (Figs. 3-11) y van YA INSERTADAS en el documento, cada una tras el párrafo que la cita por primera vez; los PDF vectoriales están en el repositorio para producción.insértelas en los puntos marcados 〈Fig. N about here〉.\nCambios de V4 a V5, por si desea revisarlos primero: (1) la regla que activa la vía de covariables se re-deriva ahora SOLO sobre validación (§3.7, Ec. 6) — en V4 se había elegido mirando el test, lo que era fuga de selección; (2) el ranking del ensemble se compara like-for-like en la Tabla 8b (9º de 12 en GEFCom2014, no 4º de 14); (3) §5.3 corrige los signos y significancias del test DM; (4) §5.6 corrige la lectura del mapa de atención (74% pasado / 26% futuro en GEFCom2014); (5) recuento real de parámetros en AEMO (320.663); (6) nueva §5.7 de amenazas a la validez. Las ecuaciones están en texto plano Unicode; conviértalas a objetos de ecuación de Word antes del envío.', size=9.0, italic=True)
par('Adaptive dual-stage signal decomposition and Patch Transformer–BiGRU with cross-attention for short-term load forecasting in smart grids: a leakage-free causal framework', align='CENTER', size=15.0, bold=True, italic=False)
par('Francisco M. Arrabal-Campos a,*', align='CENTER', size=11.0, bold=False, italic=False)
par('a CIAIMBITAL Research Centre, Department of Engineering, Universidad de Almería, 04120 Almería, Spain', align='CENTER', size=9.0, bold=False, italic=True)
par('* Corresponding author. E-mail: fmarrabal@ual.es', align='CENTER', size=9.0, bold=False, italic=False)
par('Code (PyTorch and MATLAB): https://github.com/fmarrabal/load_forecasting_smart_grids', align='CENTER', size=9.0, bold=False, italic=False)
par('Highlights', style='Heading 1')
par('Causal in-model dual-stage decomposition removes look-ahead leakage in STLF', style='List Bullet')
par('The decompose-then-split bias of CEEMDAN–VMD pipelines is quantified', style='List Bullet')
par('A full-resolution covariate path lets temperature reach the day-ahead forecast', style='List Bullet')
par('Lowest MAPE on two univariate benchmarks; no baseline beats it significantly', style='List Bullet')
par('Five-seed protocol with Holm-corrected Diebold–Mariano tests on three benchmarks', style='List Bullet')
par('Abstract', style='Heading 1')
par('Hybrid models that couple signal decomposition with deep networks dominate the recent short-term load forecasting (STLF) literature, yet most of them decompose the complete series before the chronological train–test split. Because empirical-mode and variational-mode decompositions are global transforms, this practice injects future information into every training subsequence and inflates reported accuracy far beyond what is attainable in operation. This paper first quantifies that bias on public load data and then proposes a framework that preserves the benefits of adaptive dual-stage decomposition while remaining strictly causal. The offline CEEMDAN–sample-entropy–VMD chain is replaced by an in-model, end-to-end learnable analogue: a multi-scale causal moving-average split (stage 1), a learnable causal band-pass FIR filter bank applied to the high-frequency component (stage 2), and a differentiable complexity gate that adaptively re-weights components, mirroring the role of sample-entropy routing. Components are tokenized into day-length patches, encoded by a Transformer, fused with past and future known covariates through cross-attention, and decoded by a bidirectional GRU with reversible instance normalization; a per-component linear base initialized at the weekly-persistence solution anchors the network, the deep branch learns residual corrections, and a full-resolution future-covariate path lets a strong exogenous driver such as temperature reach the forecast directly. A single 0.22–0.32M-parameter network replaces the ~30 sub-models of the offline pipeline. On three public benchmarks (GEFCom2014, PJM, AEMO), evaluated in the operational day-ahead setting over five seeds, the framework attains the lowest MAPE of all fourteen models on the two univariate benchmarks — 4.89% on PJM and 5.54% on AEMO — while on the temperature-driven GEFCom2014 it reaches 4.73%, mid-table by point error yet statistically indistinguishable from the leaders: across all three datasets no baseline outperforms it at the Holm-corrected 5% level. An ablation isolates why, and the answer is not the one the decomposition literature would predict — on the covariate-rich benchmark the covariate pathways carry the accuracy while the decomposition machinery is neutral, even though the same architecture leads both univariate benchmarks. A controlled experiment holding the learner, the features and the splits fixed and varying only the decomposition protocol shows the invalid one reporting 46-51% lower error on both benchmarks tested, confirming that leakage, not architecture, explains much of the accuracy reported by offline decomposition hybrids. Code in PyTorch and MATLAB is publicly available.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('Keywords: short-term load forecasting; smart grid; signal decomposition; data leakage; Patch Transformer; cross-attention; Diebold–Mariano test', bold=False, italic=True)
par('Nomenclature', style='Heading 1')
table(['Symbol / acronym', 'Meaning'], [['STLF', 'Short-term load forecasting'], ['CEEMDAN', 'Complete ensemble empirical mode decomposition with adaptive noise'], ['VMD', 'Variational mode decomposition'], ['SE', 'Sample entropy'], ['IMF', 'Intrinsic mode function'], ['CPTB', 'Causal-decomposition Patch Transformer–BiGRU (proposed)'], ['RevIN', 'Reversible instance normalization'], ['EC', 'Error correction (causal)'], ['DM', 'Diebold–Mariano test statistic'], ['L', 'Input window length (168 h / 336 half-hours)'], ['H', 'Forecast horizon (24 h / 48 half-hours)'], ['P', 'Patch length (24 h / 48 half-hours)'], ['N = L/P', 'Number of input patches (7)'], ['M', 'Number of decomposition components (8)'], ['K', 'Number of learnable band-pass filters (4)'], ['xₜ', 'Load at time t; ẑ denotes the RevIN-normalized window'], ['MAₖ', 'Causal moving average of kernel k (Eq. 1)'], ['cᵢ', 'i-th decomposition component'], ['hₖ / bₖ', 'k-th learnable FIR band-pass filter / its band output (Eq. 2)'], ['r', 'Reconstruction remainder channel (Eq. 2)'], ['gᵢ', 'Adaptive gate for component i (Eq. 3)'], ['Wₘ', 'Per-component linear map (window → horizon), Eq. (4)'], ['ŷ_cov', 'Full-resolution future-covariate correction, Eq. (5)'], ['N_f', 'Number of future-covariate patches (4)'], ['dₘ', 'Token embedding dimension (64)'], ['n', 'Number of non-overlapping day-ahead test forecasts (DM sample size)'], ['MAE / RMSE / MAPE / sMAPE / R²', 'Evaluation metrics']], caption=None)
par('1. Introduction', style='Heading 1')
par('Accurate short-term load forecasting underpins unit commitment, reserve sizing, demand-response scheduling and market bidding in modern power systems. As variable renewables, electrified transport and distributed storage increase both the volatility of net demand and the cost of forecast errors, day-ahead accuracy improvements of even a fraction of a percentage point translate into substantial operational savings for system operators and retailers [1,2]. The load signal itself is non-stationary, non-linear in weather drivers, and multi-scale: daily and weekly cycles are superimposed on seasonal drift and on stochastic fluctuations driven by weather fronts and calendar events.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('A prolific branch of the literature addresses this multi-scale structure with signal decomposition: the series is split into simpler sub-signals — typically with EMD-family methods such as CEEMDAN [3,4], variational mode decomposition (VMD) [5], or their combination guided by sample entropy [6] — each sub-signal is forecast separately, and the forecasts are summed. Reported gains over non-decomposed baselines routinely exceed 40–60%. A growing body of work, however, has established that most of these gains are an artefact of an invalid evaluation protocol [7–14]: when the decomposition is computed over the complete series before the chronological split (“decompose-then-split”), every training subsequence contains information from the test period, because EMD sifting and VMD are global transforms whose output at time t depends on the entire record. The resulting models cannot be reproduced in operation, where future samples do not exist at issue time. Yang et al. [12] show that a leaky CEEMDAN–BiLSTM loses an order of magnitude of its apparent accuracy once the leak is closed; similar findings are reported for hydrology [8,9], wind and solar power [7,10], and generic time series [11,13,14].', bold=False, italic=False)
par('The earlier versions of this work (and the code released with them) suffered from exactly this defect, which we diagnose openly in Section 5.4: under decompose-then-split, our CEEMDAN–SE–VMD pipeline with a patch Transformer–BiGRU learner reported 3.34% MAPE on GEFCom2014 against 8.5% for the best raw-signal baseline — an apparent improvement of about 60% that does not survive a causal protocol. That 60% is an improvement over baselines, not a measurement of the leak itself; isolating the protocol alone gives 46-51% (Section 5.4). Rather than abandoning decomposition, this paper asks a more constructive question: how much of the benefit of adaptive dual-stage decomposition can be retained when every operation is restricted to information available at forecast issue time?', bold=False, italic=False)
par('1.1. Contributions', style='Heading 2')
par('A causal, end-to-end learnable analogue of the CEEMDAN–SE–VMD chain: a multi-scale causal moving-average split (stage 1), a learnable causal band-pass FIR filter bank initialized from windowed-sinc prototypes (stage 2), and a differentiable complexity gate replacing sample-entropy K-means routing. The decomposition is computed inside each input window, reconstructs it exactly, and is trained jointly with the forecaster.', style='List Bullet')
par('A compact forecasting architecture (CPTB) that couples per-component patch tokenization, a Transformer encoder over the component×patch token grid, cross-attention fusion of covariates, a per-component linear base initialized at the weekly-persistence solution, a zero-initialized full-resolution covariate path that lets temperature reach the forecast directly, and a BiGRU decoder with reversible instance normalization — one 0.22–0.32M-parameter model per dataset instead of one model per sub-signal (~30 models totalling 4.66M parameters on GEFCom2014 and 6.37M on PJM, in the offline pipeline).', style='List Bullet')
par('A strictly causal error-correction module trained on validation errors and fed only with errors of forecasts already realized at issue time.', style='List Bullet')
par('A rigorous evaluation protocol: operational day-ahead forecasting (non-overlapping windows), train-only preprocessing statistics, five seeds, Diebold–Mariano significance tests, seasonal-naïve, DLinear and PatchTST baselines, and unmodified test targets.', style='List Bullet')
par('A controlled quantification of the decompose-then-split bias on public load data, comparing identical learners under the invalid and the causal protocol (Section 5.4).', style='List Bullet')
par('The remainder of the paper is organized as follows. Section 2 reviews related work. Section 3 details the proposed framework and its causality guarantees. Section 4 describes datasets, baselines and the evaluation protocol. Section 5 reports results, ablations, significance tests, the leakage quantification and computational cost. Section 6 concludes.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('2. Related work', style='Heading 1')
par('2.1. Decomposition-based forecasting and the decompose-then-split pitfall', style='Heading 2')
par('EMD [3] decomposes a non-stationary signal into intrinsic mode functions without a fixed basis; CEEMDAN [4] stabilizes the procedure with adaptive noise. VMD [5] formulates decomposition as a variational problem yielding band-limited modes. Dual-stage designs apply VMD to the high-frequency IMFs selected by sample entropy [6]. All of these transforms are global: appending one sample changes all components. Wang and Wu [7] first documented that EMD-based hybrid forecasters are impractical because IMFs cannot be computed without future data; Du et al. [8] demonstrated “incorrect high performance” in SSA/DWT hybrids; Quilty and Adamowski [9] formalized the misuse modes and proposed boundary-corrected causal frameworks; Safari et al. [10] analyzed the effect for load and renewables; Zhang et al. [11] named the flawed sampling scheme and proposed fully stepwise decomposition; Yang et al. [12] measured order-of-magnitude accuracy loss in CEEMDAN hybrids once leakage is removed; Cai and Li [13] reviewed rolling-decomposition ensembles and their boundary and cost problems; VMDNet [14] performs sample-wise VMD strictly within each input window. The consensus remedy is to confine any decomposition to the input window (or to learn it end-to-end), which is the route taken here. In-model learnable decompositions in Autoformer [15] and FEDformer [16] (moving-average trend–seasonal blocks) are causal in exactly this sense.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('2.2. Deep architectures for load forecasting', style='Heading 2')
par('Recurrent networks (LSTM [17], GRU [18]) remain strong sequence baselines; temporal convolutional networks [19] replace recurrence with dilated causal convolutions; Transformers [20] with patch tokenization (PatchTST [21]) reduce attention cost while preserving local semantics — a day-length patch is the natural token for load data. Linear models with series decomposition (DLinear [22]) are competitive with far larger Transformers and are now mandatory baselines; iTransformer [23] attends across variates; TimesNet [24] models period-unfolded 2-D variations; frequency-domain MLPs [25] offer spectral modelling without offline decomposition. Reversible instance normalization (RevIN) [26] mitigates distribution shift per window and removes the need for globally fitted scalers — an additional leakage safeguard. Gradient-boosted trees (XGBoost [27], LightGBM [28]) remain strong tabular baselines for STLF.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('2.3. Covariate fusion and known-future inputs', style='Heading 2')
par('Day-ahead forecasting benefits from covariates that are known for the horizon: calendar identity and, where available, weather forecasts. The Temporal Fusion Transformer [29] and TiDE [30] make known-future inputs first-class citizens; TSMixer [31] mixes auxiliary channels in an all-MLP design. In load forecasting, however, most decomposition hybrids concatenate past covariates only. Cross-attention — the load representation querying a covariate stream — offers a principled and interpretable fusion mechanism; combining it with known-future calendar tokens is, to our knowledge, unexplored within causal decomposition frameworks for STLF.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('2.4. Positioning', style='Heading 2')
par('No published STLF framework combines (i) a causal, learnable dual-stage decomposition with exact reconstruction, (ii) patch-level Transformer encoding over the component×patch grid, (iii) cross-attention fusion of past and known-future covariates, and (iv) a leakage-audited evaluation protocol with significance testing. Equally rare is an explicit quantification of the bias the causal design removes; we provide both.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('3. Proposed methodology', style='Heading 1')
par('3.1. Problem formulation', style='Heading 2')
par('Let xₜ denote the load at time t on a regular grid of s steps per day (s = 24 hourly, s = 48 half-hourly). At issue time t₀ the forecaster receives the past window x[t₀−L : t₀), past covariates U[t₀−L : t₀) and future known covariates V[t₀ : t₀+H) (calendar features; temperature proxy where available), and must output ŷ[t₀ : t₀+H). We use L = 7s (one week) and H = s (one day). Operationally, forecasts are issued once per day, so consecutive test windows do not overlap. Every quantity used below is measurable at issue time from past observations — with the single, explicitly stated exception of the horizon temperature proxy on GEFCom2014 (Section 4.2), which stands in for a day-ahead weather forecast in the GEFCom tradition and is supplied identically to all models. The decomposition’s position-wise causality is verified by an automated test, included in both the Python and MATLAB releases, that perturbs the most recent input sample and checks that no earlier decomposition output changes.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('3.2. Framework overview', style='Heading 2')
par('Fig. 1 contrasts the conventional decompose-then-split pipeline with the proposed causal protocol, and Fig. 2 gives the model’s data flow with tensor shapes. The normalized input window is decomposed causally into M = 8 components that sum to the window exactly (Section 3.3); an example decomposition of a single input window is shown in Fig. 3. Each component is tokenized into N = 7 day-length patches (Section 3.4). A Transformer encoder processes the 56-token component×patch grid; component tokens are then averaged per patch position into a 7-token temporal sequence, which queries past- and future-covariate tokens via cross-attention (Section 3.5). A per-component linear base maps the decomposition directly to the horizon (Section 3.6) and, where the validation rule of Eq. (6) enables it, a full-resolution covariate path adds a direct covariate correction (Section 3.7); a BiGRU decodes the fused sequence and a two-layer head adds a residual correction, denormalized by RevIN (Section 3.8). An optional causal error-correction stage refines the forecast using only realized errors (Section 3.9).', bold=False, italic=False)
figure(FIG_FILES[1])
par('Fig. 1. Where the leakage comes from. (a) The conventional pipeline fits CEEMDAN/VMD on the complete series before the chronological split, so information from the test period flows back into every training window; the measured consequence is an apparent accuracy gain of 45.9 % that vanishes under a causal protocol (Section 5.4). (b) The proposed framework splits first and decomposes causally inside each input window, which uses only data observable at the issue time t₀. Generated by figures_diagrams.py.', size=9.0, bold=False, italic=False)
figure(FIG_FILES[2])
par('Fig. 2. Architecture of the proposed CPTB model with tensor shapes for GEFCom2014 (L = 168, H = 24, M = 8 components, N = 7 patches, d = 64). Green: the causal dual-stage decomposition and the per-component linear base; blue: the patch Transformer–BiGRU pathway; orange: the covariate pathways. The deep branch, the linear base and the covariate path are summed before the RevIN inverse. Generated by figures_diagrams.py.', size=9.0, bold=False, italic=False)
figure(FIG_FILES[3])
par('Fig. 3. The causal dual-stage decomposition applied to one GEFCom2014 input window by the trained model: the four learnable band-pass outputs and the remainder (green) together with the stage-1 intra-day, weekly and trend scales (blue). The components sum back to the window to within 2.4·10⁻⁷. Panels are autoscaled so that each shape is legible, so the figure at the right of each gives that component’s share of the total component standard deviation: the weekly scale (31.3 %) and the remainder (22.0 %) dominate, while the four learned bands together account for 19.5 % — consistent with the ablation of Section 5.2, where removing the filter bank costs almost nothing on this benchmark. Generated by figures_results.py.', size=9.0, bold=False, italic=False)
par('〈Fig. 1 about here〉      〈Fig. 2 about here〉', align='CENTER', bold=False, italic=True)
par('3.3. Causal adaptive dual-stage decomposition', style='Heading 2')
par('3.3.1. Stage 1: multi-scale causal moving-average split', style='Heading 3')
par('With ẑ the RevIN-normalized window and MAₖ the causal moving average of kernel k (replicate left padding), MAₖ[t] = (1/k) Σⱼ₌₀..ₖ₋₁ ẑ[t−j], the window is split with kernels k ∈ {s/2, s, 7s} into', bold=False, italic=False)
par('c₁ = ẑ − MA_{s/2},  c₂ = MA_{s/2} − MA_s,  c₃ = MA_s − MA_{7s},  c₄ = MA_{7s},        (1)', align='CENTER', bold=False, italic=False)
par('isolating intra-day high-frequency content, sub-daily cycles, the weekly band and the trend. By construction Σ cᵢ = ẑ (exact reconstruction) and every output at position t depends only on positions ≤ t (strict causality). This stage plays the role of CEEMDAN in the offline pipeline: a data-adaptive coarse separation of time scales, in the spirit of the decomposition blocks of Autoformer/FEDformer [15,16] but multi-scale and reconstruction-exact.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('3.3.2. Stage 2: learnable causal band-pass filter bank', style='Heading 3')
par('VMD is, at its core, adaptive band-pass filtering [5]. Its causal, learnable counterpart is a bank of K = 4 FIR filters hₖ of length 25 applied to the high-frequency component c₁ with zero left padding:', bold=False, italic=False)
par('bₖ[t] = Σⱼ hₖ[j] · c₁[t−j],   k = 1..K,   r = c₁ − Σₖ bₖ,        (2)', align='CENTER', bold=False, italic=False)
par('where the remainder channel r guarantees exact reconstruction regardless of the learned weights. Filters are initialized as Hamming-windowed sinc band-pass prototypes on K equal sub-bands of (0, π) and are trained jointly with the network, so the band edges adapt to the data — the “adaptive” property of VMD — while remaining strictly causal FIR operations. The final component set is {b₁..b₄, r, c₂, c₃, c₄}, M = 8.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('3.3.3. Differentiable complexity gating', style='Heading 3')
par('The offline pipeline routed IMFs by sample entropy and K-means — a non-differentiable decision taken on the full series. We replace it with a per-window, per-component gate: from causal statistics of each component (log-energy, roughness — the mean absolute first difference over the standard deviation, an entropy proxy — and energy share), a two-layer MLP produces gᵢ ∈ (0, 2) that scales the component’s token embeddings:', bold=False, italic=False)
par('gᵢ = 2σ(MLP[log Eᵢ, Rᵢ, Sᵢ]).        (3)', align='CENTER', bold=False, italic=False)
par('Components the network finds informative are amplified and noisy ones attenuated, per window, differentiably, and without any global fit.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('3.4. Patch tokenization and Transformer encoding', style='Heading 2')
par('Each component is segmented into N = L/P non-overlapping patches of length P = s (one day). A shared linear projection maps each patch to dₘ = 64 dimensions; sinusoidal positional encodings mark the patch position and a learnable component embedding marks the component identity. The resulting M×N = 56 tokens are processed by a two-layer pre-norm Transformer encoder (4 heads, feed-forward width 128, GELU, dropout 0.1), which lets patches exchange information both across time and across components. Token states are then averaged over components at each patch position, yielding an N-token temporal sequence.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('3.5. Cross-attention fusion of past and future covariates', style='Heading 2')
par('Past covariates (calendar features and, on GEFCom2014, temperature) are averaged per patch and projected to dₘ, giving N past tokens; future known covariates for the horizon are grouped into N_f = 4 patches of H/4 steps, flattened, projected, and marked with a learnable future-type embedding. The temporal sequence queries the concatenated covariate tokens through multi-head cross-attention (2 heads) with a residual connection and layer normalization. The attention matrix (N × (N+N_f)) is directly interpretable: it shows which past days and which segments of the forecast day condition each part of the load representation (Section 5.6). On univariate datasets the covariate stream contains calendar features only, so the module remains active and meaningful — unlike in V3, where its role on those datasets was undefined.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('3.6. Per-component linear base and deep residual correction', style='Heading 2')
par('Direct linear maps from the input window to the horizon are remarkably strong forecasters on smooth, persistence-dominated load series [22]. Rather than competing with this hypothesis class, the framework contains it: each decomposition component cₘ receives its own linear map Wₘ ∈ ℝ^{H×L}, and the base forecast is', bold=False, italic=False)
par('ŷ_base = Σₘ Wₘ cₘ + b,        (4)', align='CENTER', bold=False, italic=False)
par('to which the deep branch of Sections 3.4–3.5 adds a residual correction. Two initialization choices make this construction well-behaved. First, every Wₘ is initialized as the seasonal-naive selector (Wₘ[h, h] = 1; recall L equals one week, so window position h is the same hour one week before horizon step h). Because the components sum to the window exactly, the initial base equals the weekly-persistence forecast. Second, the final layer of the deep head is zero-initialized, so the untrained model outputs exactly the seasonal-naive forecast and gradient descent can only improve on it — a property verified by an automated test in both released implementations. The linear base generalizes DLinear [22]: where DLinear maps two components (trend, remainder) through linear layers, the base here maps eight causally band-separated components, while the deep branch contributes what no linear map can express — temperature nonlinearity, calendar interactions and regime-dependent corrections.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('3.7. Full-resolution covariate path', style='Heading 2')
par('The cross-attention of Section 3.5 averages covariates per day-length patch before fusing them, which is adequate for calendar features but discards the hourly resolution of temperature — the dominant predictor on days driven by heating and cooling demand. The ablation of Section 5.2 shows this is the single largest weakness of the patch-only design on the temperature-rich benchmark. We therefore add a direct covariate path: the full-resolution future known covariates V[t₀:t₀+H] are flattened and mapped to an H-step additive correction by a two-layer MLP whose final layer is zero-initialized,', bold=False, italic=False)
par('ŷ_cov = W₂ · GELU(W₁ · vec(V)) ,        (5)', align='CENTER', bold=False, italic=False)
par('added to the forecast alongside the linear base of Eq. (4). Being zero-initialized it is neutral at the start of training and preserves the seasonal-naive initialization; it is the same additive, zero-initialized injection that keeps the DLinear baseline competitive. This path is the main driver of the framework’s accuracy on GEFCom2014 (Section 5.2).', bold=False, italic=False)
par('Whether the path is active is the single configuration choice the framework makes per dataset, and we state the selection procedure explicitly because a paper about leakage should not be casual about it. The rule is', bold=False, italic=False)
par('enable the covariate path on a dataset iff it lowers the mean VALIDATION MAPE of the proposed model on that dataset,        (6)', align='CENTER', bold=False, italic=False)
par('evaluated over three seeds with the test split never touched. The released script select_covskip_on_val.py reproduces the comparison and writes its six validation scores to results/covskip_selection.json, which is the file the table below is printed from and which a reader can check against the released configuration; the rule enables the path on GEFCom2014 and disables it on the two univariate benchmarks. That outcome coincides with the configuration the earlier, test-informed rule had produced. We report this plainly: the numbers did not change, the way they were arrived at did, and only the second version is defensible.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('Table 1. The selection rule of Eq. (6), evaluated on the VALIDATION split only (mean ± std over seeds 42–44). Negative Δ means the full-resolution covariate path lowers validation error and is therefore enabled. No test data enters this decision.', size=10.0, bold=True)
table(['Dataset', 'Val. MAPE with the path (%)', 'Val. MAPE without it (%)', 'Δ (pp)', 'Selected'], [['GEFCom2014', '4.766 ± 0.182', '5.091 ± 0.100', '−0.325', 'enabled'], ['PJM', '3.605 ± 0.119', '3.421 ± 0.055', '+0.184', 'disabled'], ['AEMO', '5.359 ± 0.064', '5.240 ± 0.063', '+0.119', 'disabled']], caption=None)
par('An earlier version of this work tied the same switch to the mere presence of temperature, a rule that had in fact been chosen after inspecting test-set scores. That is selection leakage — a slower, subtler form of the very defect Section 5.4 quantifies — and replacing it with a validation-only rule is what makes the reported test figures genuinely held out. That the two rules happen to agree is fortunate but beside the point: had they disagreed, the validation rule would have won and the affected dataset would have been re-run. We flag this openly: leakage does not only enter through preprocessing, it also enters through the decisions an author makes while looking at results.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('3.8. BiGRU decoding, forecast head and normalization', style='Heading 2')
par('A single bidirectional GRU layer (64 units per direction) over the N fused tokens supplies the sequential inductive bias that self-attention lacks. The concatenation of its final state, its mean-pooled states and the mean future covariate token feeds a two-layer MLP (width 128, GELU, dropout 0.2) whose output is added to the linear base of Eq. (4) and, where enabled, the covariate path of Eq. (5), in normalized space; RevIN [26] statistics of the input window (mean, standard deviation, learnable affine) denormalize the sum. RevIN also removes the need for any globally fitted target scaler, closing the scaler-leakage channel of V3 entirely. The complete model has 254,527 parameters on GEFCom2014 (including the covariate path), 216,167 on PJM and 320,663 on AEMO; the AEMO figure is larger because the per-component linear base scales as M·H·L and the half-hourly resolution doubles both H and L. The offline pipeline instead trained one model per sub-signal, totalling 4.66M parameters on GEFCom2014 and 6.37M on PJM, so the single causal model is 18× and 29× smaller respectively; it is trained end to end with Huber loss (δ = 1).', bold=False, italic=False)
par('3.9. Causal error correction', style='Heading 2')
par('Systematic errors (e.g., holiday effects) can persist across days. We train a small GRU (32 units, 12k parameters) that maps the last W = 168 realized errors to an H-step additive correction. Under the operational protocol, the errors of every forecast issued at or before t₀ − H are fully observed at issue time t₀, so the input never contains unobserved information. The module is trained on validation-set errors only and evaluated as an ablation (Section 5.2); unlike V3 — where the correction stage read test-set ground truth and was trained on temporally scrambled residuals — both its inputs and its training signal are operationally available. Because the validation set also selects the early-stopping checkpoint, the correction is trained on the errors of a validation-optimal model; this biases the reported benefit downward (the correction transfers to a less-favorable test error distribution), so any improvement it shows is conservative, and its contribution is significance-tested rather than asserted.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('3.10. Causality guarantees', style='Heading 2')
par('Every stage satisfies a window property: all quantities used to produce ŷ[t₀:t₀+H) are functions of data observable at t₀. Preprocessing statistics (z-score parameters, outlier bounds) are fitted on the training segment only; the decomposition and gating operate inside the input window; RevIN uses window statistics; future covariates are deterministic calendar functions (plus a temperature proxy whose use is stated explicitly in Section 4.2); the error-correction input lags by at least H. The released code contains an automated test that perturbs the most recent input sample and verifies that no decomposition output at earlier positions changes — a machine-checkable certificate of causality.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('4. Experimental setup', style='Heading 1')
par('4.1. Datasets', style='Heading 2')
par('Three public benchmarks with different market structures, resolutions and covariate availability are used (Table 2). GEFCom2014-L is the load track of the Global Energy Forecasting Competition 2014 [1]: hourly utility-zone load (in the anonymized units of the competition files) with 25 weather stations averaged into one temperature signal. PJM (PJME region) provides seventeen years of hourly system load in MW. AEMO provides five years of half-hourly operational demand (MW) for New South Wales. From October 2021 the same portal also publishes a 5-minute dispatch series, and a download may contain both; the two are different quantities — resampled to half-hours they differ by about 104 MW on average — so the sub-half-hourly records are excluded rather than aggregated, leaving the half-hourly trading-interval demand complete and gap-free. PJM contains 4 duplicated timestamps and 30 missing hours at daylight-saving transitions, which are averaged and interpolated respectively. GEFCom2014 is the only benchmark with substantial gaps: 3,839 of its 52,055 hours are absent from the source file, including multi-week blocks in 2010, and every window overlapping them is excluded from training and from evaluation (Section 4.2).', bold=False, italic=False)
par('Table 2. Dataset statistics, recomputed from the released files by data_utils.py. Every quantity refers to the series after reindexing to a strictly regular grid, which is the series the models see: the grid is longer than the source file wherever timestamps are missing, and the second row separates genuine observations from gap-filled steps. Load statistics use observed steps only and precede outlier treatment. The split boundaries are the 70/15/15 points of the gridded series. The test-forecast count n is the number of non-overlapping day-ahead issue times whose full target window is genuinely observed; it is the sample size of every Diebold–Mariano test.', size=10.0, bold=True)
table(['Property', 'GEFCom2014', 'PJM (PJME)', 'AEMO NSW'], [['Resolution', 'hourly', 'hourly', 'half-hourly'], ['Steps on the regular grid', '52,055', '145,392', '87,696'], ['— observed / gap-filled', '48,216 / 3,839', '145,362 / 30', '87,696 / 0'], ['Period', '2005-01-01 – 2010-12-09', '2002-01-01 – 2018-08-03', '2020-01-01 – 2025-01-01'], ['Load mean ± std', '145.4 ± 46.5', '32,080 ± 6,464 MW', '7,565 ± 1,329 MW'], ['Load min / max', '48.4 / 315.6', '14,544 / 62,009 MW', '3,083 / 13,737 MW'], ['Temperature', 'yes (25 stations, avg.)', 'no', 'no'], ['Train ends / Val ends', '2009-02-27 / 2010-01-18', '2013-08-11 / 2016-02-06', '2023-07-02 / 2024-04-01'], ['L / H / P (steps)', '168 / 24 / 24', '168 / 24 / 24', '336 / 48 / 48'], ['Day-ahead test forecasts (n)', '259', '902', '274']], caption=None)
par('4.2. Preprocessing and leakage hygiene', style='Heading 2')
par('Short gaps (≤ 6 steps) are linearly interpolated. Outlier bounds (3×IQR) are computed on the training segment and replacements (a trailing daily median) applied to the training segment only — validation and test targets are never modified, unlike V3 where IQR replacement over the full series altered the test ground truth. GEFCom2014 additionally contains several multi-week blocks of missing hours (notably 31 and 21 consecutive days in 2010, within the test period); rather than evaluating any model against gap-filled synthetic load, we mark every genuinely observed timestamp and exclude from all trained-on, early-stopping and evaluated windows any issue time whose forecast horizon overlaps a fabricated step (and, so that the two-sided interpolation cannot leak a target value backward, whose final six input steps are not all observed). This removes 66 of 325 candidate day-ahead windows on GEFCom2014 (a few on PJM, none on AEMO), leaving the test-forecast counts n reported in Table 2. The load z-score scaler is fitted on the training segment; the proposed model additionally applies RevIN per window. Calendar covariates are cyclical encodings of hour, day of week and month, a weekend flag and a fixed-date holiday proxy. On GEFCom2014, actual temperature is supplied for the forecast horizon as a perfect-forecast proxy — a standard, explicitly stated simplification [1] — together with heating/cooling degree features max(0, T_ref − T) and max(0, T − T_ref), where T_ref is the training-set median temperature (a train-only statistic, hence causal). These encode the V-shaped load–temperature relation explicitly. All models receive the identical covariate set, so comparisons are unaffected. Because this proxy uses the realized horizon temperature, the GEFCom2014 accuracy figures are upper bounds with respect to weather-forecast uncertainty; the univariate PJM and AEMO results carry no such caveat, and model rankings are unaffected since the proxy is shared.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('4.3. Baselines', style='Heading 2')
par('Thirteen baselines span all model families: seasonal-naïve (weekly persistence — the honest floor any method must beat), seasonal ARIMA (refit on a 4-week history for each test day), XGBoost [27] and LightGBM [28] (one regressor per horizon step, with future calendar features), LSTM [17], BiLSTM, GRU [18], TCN [19] (causal, left-padded), vanilla Transformer [20], CNN–LSTM, GRU–TCN–Attention, DLinear [22] and PatchTST [21]. All deep baselines receive the same inputs as the proposed model — past load, past covariates and future known covariates. Future covariates enter each recurrent/convolutional/transformer backbone through a shared conditioning head that fuses the backbone’s state with the flattened future covariates; for DLinear, which is defined as a pure linear map, they instead enter as an additive, zero-initialized correction so its linear pathway is kept at full strength rather than throttled (the earlier fixed damping is removed). The information set is therefore identical across models; we do not claim mechanism equivalence, and a covariate-native architecture (e.g. TiDE, TFT) is a natural further baseline. Unlike V3, the decomposition-based competitor is not a leaky offline hybrid but the causal framework itself, ablated in Section 5.2.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('Table 3. Hyperparameters (identical across datasets; no per-dataset tuning).', size=10.0, bold=True)
table(['Group', 'Setting', 'Value'], [['Model', 'd_model / heads / encoder layers / FFN', '64 / 4 / 2 / 128'], ['Model', 'BiGRU hidden per direction', '64'], ['Model', 'Stage-1 kernels (steps, hourly)', '12, 24, 168'], ['Model', 'Stage-2 filters K / length', '4 / 25'], ['Model', 'Patch length P / future patches N_f', 's / 4'], ['Model', 'Dropout (transformer / BiGRU / head)', '0.1 / 0.2 / 0.2'], ['Model', 'Linear-base init / deep-head final-layer init', 'seasonal-naive selector / zeros'], ['Features', 'Degree-day covariates (T_ref = train median)', 'HDD, CDD (temperature datasets)'], ['Training', 'Optimizer / lr / weight decay', 'Adam / 10⁻³ / 10⁻⁵'], ['Training', 'Schedule / epochs / patience', 'cosine to 10⁻⁶ / 60 / 10'], ['Training', 'Batch / loss / grad clip', '64 / Huber (δ=1) / 1.0'], ['Protocol', 'Seeds', '42–46 (5 runs, mean ± std)'], ['Protocol', 'Test stride', 'H (operational day-ahead)'], ['EC', 'GRU hidden / error window W / epochs', '32 / 168 / 50']], caption=None)
par('4.4. Evaluation protocol', style='Heading 2')
par('Validation and test forecasts are issued every H steps (one per day), exactly as in operation; overlapping stride-1 evaluation — used in V3 — inflates sample counts with autocorrelated windows and biases significance tests. Metrics (MAE, RMSE, MAPE, sMAPE, R²) are computed on the original scale against genuinely observed targets; we report MAE and RMSE alongside MAPE because MAPE has known biases on load data [35]. Each deep model is trained with five seeds; tables report mean ± sample standard deviation. Significance is assessed with the Diebold–Mariano test [32]. Because forecasts are non-overlapping (one per day), the loss-differential series is tested with a Newey-West (Bartlett) HAC variance at the automatic bandwidth ⌊4(n/100)^{2/9}⌋ and the Harvey–Leybourne–Newbold small-sample correction [33] at unit horizon — not the H-step convention that applies only to overlapping forecasts. The headline test operates on the five-seed mean per-issue loss profile (the quantity the tables report), avoiding the pseudo-replication of stacking 5·n differentials; the single-seed statistic is given alongside for reference. Both squared-error and absolute-error loss variants are reported, and p-values are Holm–Bonferroni adjusted within each dataset’s family of comparisons to control the family-wise error rate across the thirteen baselines. Seed ensembling (the mean of the five seeds’ forecasts) is evaluated symmetrically: every model’s ensemble is available and the proposed ensemble is compared only against baseline ensembles, never against single-model baselines. This protocol follows the open-benchmark best practices for energy forecasting [34]; the test window is 2.5 years on PJM and roughly one year on GEFCom2014 and AEMO under the fixed 70/15/15 split.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('4.5. Implementation', style='Heading 2')
par('The framework is implemented twice — in PyTorch and in MATLAB (Deep Learning Toolbox, custom dlnetwork training loop) — with identical semantics, and both implementations are released. Experiments were run on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 5000 Blackwell GPU (48 GB) with PyTorch 2.11/CUDA 12.8; the proposed model trains in 6.2 min per seed on GEFCom2014 and 4.9 min on AEMO, and in 28.3 min on PJM, whose series is three times longer; the five-seed protocol therefore costs 31 min, 24 min and 2.4 h per dataset respectively (Table 10). All preprocessing, training, statistical testing, tables and figures are reproduced by two commands (main.py, leakage_demo.py). Every figure is generated from a result file, and the code raises rather than substituting a default when one is missing — a discipline we adopted only after an audit of this release found Fig. 11 and the leakage annotation of Fig. 1 being drawn from literals typed into the figure code, because leakage_demo.py printed its measurement without persisting it. The placeholder-based figures of V3 (an ablation chart and error-correction histograms rendered from hardcoded or random values) are what that discipline exists to prevent, and the failure mode evidently recurs unless it is enforced mechanically rather than intended.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('5. Results and discussion', style='Heading 1')
par('5.1. Forecasting accuracy', style='Heading 2')
par("On the two univariate benchmarks the proposed framework attains the lowest MAPE of the fourteen models. The claim needs qualifying by metric, and we make it explicit rather than leave the reader to notice: on AEMO the model is first on all five reported metrics, but on PJM it is first on MAE, MAPE and sMAPE and fourth on RMSE and R², where DLinear (2,190 / 0.8845) and PatchTST (2,192 / 0.8843) are ahead of its 2,214 / 0.8819. Being best on the absolute-error family and mid-table on the squared-error family is a real pattern, not a rounding artefact: the model is tuned with a Huber objective and gives up something on the largest deviations. On PJM (Table 5) it attains 4.89% MAPE, below the 4.98% of the strongest baseline (PatchTST); on AEMO (Table 6) it attains 5.54%, below the 5.60% of DLinear. On the temperature-driven GEFCom2014 (Table 4) it attains 4.73%, which places it eleventh of fourteen by point error — behind the strongest deep baselines (TCN 4.13%, GRU 4.23%) — although, as Section 5.3 shows, none of those differences survives a Holm-corrected Diebold–Mariano test. Fig. 4 shows the three rankings side by side. Across all three datasets no baseline outperforms the proposed model at the 5% level after multiplicity correction; the proposed model in turn significantly beats seasonal persistence everywhere and seasonal ARIMA on GEFCom2014 and PJM (on AEMO, ARIMA's forecasts diverge so wildly — 19.2% MAPE, R² = −72 — that the squared-error differential is too volatile for the test to resolve, p_Holm = 0.94, an instructive reminder that a large point-error gap and statistical significance are not the same thing).", bold=False, italic=False)
figure(FIG_FILES[4])
par('Fig. 4. Day-ahead accuracy on the three benchmarks under the leak-free protocol (mean MAPE over five seeds), on a scale shared across the three panels. The leading models are shown per benchmark, with the proposed model always included — where it falls outside the leading group a labelled break states how many models were omitted — and its overall rank stated; it is first of fourteen on both univariate datasets and eleventh on GEFCom2014, where none of the differences is significant (Table 8).', size=9.0, bold=False, italic=False)
par("Seed ensembling (the mean of the five seeds' forecasts, a variance reduction already paid for by the multi-seed protocol) is reported separately in Table 9, where EVERY model's ensemble is ranked against every other — mixing a five-seed ensemble into the single-model ranking of Tables 4–6 would not be a like-for-like comparison. Under that comparison the proposed ensemble is first of twelve on PJM (4.75%) and on AEMO (5.22%), and ninth of twelve on GEFCom2014 (4.27% against 3.70% for the TCN ensemble); no baseline ensemble beats the proposed ensemble significantly on any dataset. The PJM ensemble rank carries the same metric caveat as above and we state it here too: it is first on MAPE but second on MAE and third on RMSE and R², behind the PatchTST ensemble (1,513 / 2,125 / 0.8912). Table 9 is sorted by MAPE for consistency with Tables 4–6, so its rank-1 row should not be read as a sweep. Figs. 5–7 overlay consecutive day-ahead forecasts per dataset, each on a representative window rather than the first or the best one.", bold=False, italic=False)
figure(FIG_FILES[5])
par('Fig. 5. Five consecutive day-ahead forecasts on the GEFCom2014 test set, with the residual below. Vertical rules mark the daily forecast boundaries: forecasts are issued once per day and never overlap. The window is not the first of the test period and not the best: it is the run of five consecutive days whose MAPE is closest to the test-set mean, and both figures are printed in the panel title so the reader can verify that it is representative. Figs. 5–7, 9 and 10 are diagnostics computed on the primary seed (42) rather than on the five-seed mean prediction, so the test MAPE printed in their titles (4.58 / 4.87 / 5.45 % on GEFCom2014, PJM and AEMO) is a single-seed quantity and is slightly below the five-seed means of Tables 4–6 (4.73 / 4.89 / 5.54 %); the titles say which is which.', size=9.0, bold=False, italic=False)
figure(FIG_FILES[6])
par('Fig. 6. The same view on PJM.', size=9.0, bold=False, italic=False)
figure(FIG_FILES[7])
par('Fig. 7. The same view on AEMO (half-hourly, H = 48; the horizontal axis counts half-hours).', size=9.0, bold=False, italic=False)
par('Fig. 10 resolves the error across the horizon, and doing so exposes a property of the protocol worth stating. Under an operational day-ahead schedule — one forecast per day, horizon equal to one day — lead time and time of day are perfectly confounded: the k-th step of every forecast always falls at the same clock time. Within a single dataset the two are therefore inseparable — a point we return to in Section 5.7(vi) — so the figure cannot decompose the error into the two contributions. It can, however, show that neither is negligible. The lead-time contribution is the discontinuity at each issue marker, where the last step of one forecast and the first step of the next lie a single time step apart on the clock (for PJM, 07:00 and 08:00): across that one step the median error falls from 2.15–4.53 % to 0.54–0.92 %, a change no time-of-day effect could produce in half an hour to an hour. The time-of-day contribution is the AEMO midday hump, whose median peaks at 10.3 % at 12:00 and stays above 7.5 % from 10:00 to 15:00 — hours in which behind-the-meter photovoltaic generation makes New South Wales net demand unusually volatile. That hump has no counterpart on the other two benchmarks, but they differ from AEMO in region, period and resolution as well as in issue hour, so this is a suggestive cross-dataset observation rather than a controlled separation. The narrow, defensible conclusion is that reporting the lead-time axis alone, as is conventional, invites the reader to attribute the whole of that hump to forecast horizon, when at least part of it is a property of the clock.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('Table 4. GEFCom2014, operational day-ahead protocol, MAPE-sorted, mean ± std over 5 seeds (deterministic baselines have no std). Single-seed models only: the five-seed ensembles are ranked against each other in Table 9, because placing one model’s ensemble in this table would compare five training runs against one.', size=10.0, bold=True)
table(['Model', 'MAE', 'RMSE', 'MAPE (%)', 'R²', 'sMAPE (%)'], [['TCN', '6.51 ± 0.27', '8.84 ± 0.30', '4.13 ± 0.13', '0.9730', '4.13'], ['GRU', '6.68 ± 0.16', '8.91 ± 0.15', '4.23 ± 0.10', '0.9725', '4.23'], ['CNN-LSTM', '6.68 ± 0.34', '8.93 ± 0.42', '4.25 ± 0.18', '0.9724', '4.24'], ['Transformer', '6.79 ± 0.41', '9.13 ± 0.47', '4.30 ± 0.19', '0.9711', '4.29'], ['LSTM', '6.81 ± 0.27', '9.16 ± 0.39', '4.31 ± 0.14', '0.9709', '4.31'], ['LightGBM', '7.22', '10.04', '4.35', '0.9651', '4.42'], ['XGBoost', '7.25', '10.13', '4.35', '0.9645', '4.42'], ['BiLSTM', '6.92 ± 0.23', '9.25 ± 0.34', '4.43 ± 0.10', '0.9704', '4.43'], ['DLinear', '6.91 ± 0.66', '9.22 ± 0.74', '4.43 ± 0.43', '0.9705', '4.50'], ['GRU-TCN-Attention', '7.28 ± 0.44', '9.77 ± 0.53', '4.59 ± 0.27', '0.9669', '4.61'], ['Proposed (CPTB)', '7.47 ± 0.20', '10.65 ± 0.48', '4.73 ± 0.11', '0.9607', '4.78'], ['PatchTST', '9.08 ± 0.33', '12.35 ± 0.39', '5.85 ± 0.26', '0.9472', '5.71'], ['ARIMA (seasonal)', '12.33', '17.83', '8.22', '0.8901', '8.02'], ['Seasonal-naïve', '27.39', '36.75', '17.51', '0.5329', '16.88']], caption='Table 5. PJM, operational day-ahead protocol (MW), mean ± std over 5 seeds.')
table(['Model', 'MAE', 'RMSE', 'MAPE (%)', 'R²', 'sMAPE (%)'], [['Proposed (CPTB)', '1559 ± 22', '2214 ± 49', '4.89 ± 0.06', '0.8819', '4.89'], ['PatchTST', '1574 ± 28', '2192 ± 35', '4.98 ± 0.08', '0.8843', '4.95'], ['DLinear', '1615 ± 18', '2190 ± 11', '5.15 ± 0.08', '0.8845', '5.10'], ['LightGBM', '1630', '2239', '5.19', '0.8792', '5.14'], ['TCN', '1635 ± 47', '2206 ± 35', '5.21 ± 0.18', '0.8827', '5.14'], ['XGBoost', '1638', '2251', '5.23', '0.8780', '5.17'], ['Transformer', '1676 ± 73', '2254 ± 39', '5.35 ± 0.30', '0.8776', '5.29'], ['CNN-LSTM', '1708 ± 48', '2342 ± 58', '5.46 ± 0.17', '0.8678', '5.36'], ['GRU-TCN-Attention', '1714 ± 71', '2325 ± 60', '5.48 ± 0.27', '0.8697', '5.38'], ['BiLSTM', '1721 ± 92', '2345 ± 126', '5.53 ± 0.32', '0.8672', '5.41'], ['GRU', '1769 ± 64', '2348 ± 45', '5.70 ± 0.25', '0.8672', '5.57'], ['ARIMA (seasonal)', '1806', '2499', '5.71', '0.8496', '5.74'], ['LSTM', '1780 ± 65', '2376 ± 69', '5.74 ± 0.23', '0.8639', '5.60'], ['Seasonal-naïve', '3440', '4743', '10.71', '0.4581', '10.59']], caption='Table 6. AEMO, operational day-ahead protocol (MW), mean ± std over 5 seeds. (Seasonal ARIMA diverges on AEMO’s half-hourly series and is omitted.)')
table(['Model', 'MAE', 'RMSE', 'MAPE (%)', 'R²', 'sMAPE (%)'], [['Proposed (CPTB)', '384 ± 7', '582 ± 10', '5.54 ± 0.13', '0.8518', '5.49'], ['DLinear', '389 ± 12', '589 ± 7', '5.60 ± 0.17', '0.8481', '5.55'], ['PatchTST', '398 ± 6', '584 ± 5', '5.79 ± 0.08', '0.8509', '5.71'], ['LightGBM', '403', '615', '6.14', '0.8344', '5.85'], ['XGBoost', '413', '635', '6.32', '0.8238', '5.98'], ['Transformer', '430 ± 19', '620 ± 20', '6.39 ± 0.22', '0.8317', '6.19'], ['GRU-TCN-Attention', '439 ± 18', '626 ± 18', '6.45 ± 0.30', '0.8287', '6.29'], ['BiLSTM', '433 ± 11', '629 ± 12', '6.50 ± 0.14', '0.8271', '6.24'], ['GRU', '431 ± 5', '627 ± 5', '6.52 ± 0.13', '0.8279', '6.22'], ['TCN', '431 ± 18', '633 ± 22', '6.56 ± 0.33', '0.8245', '6.21'], ['LSTM', '435 ± 7', '635 ± 9', '6.59 ± 0.11', '0.8237', '6.28'], ['CNN-LSTM', '448 ± 5', '653 ± 8', '6.84 ± 0.09', '0.8137', '6.47'], ['Seasonal-naïve', '627', '932', '9.05', '0.6198', '8.83']], caption=None)
par('5.2. Ablation study', style='Heading 2')
par('Each variant in Table 7 differs from the full model in exactly one component, with two stated exceptions — unlike V3, whose “component” ablations also silently removed the decomposition (and whose “w/o VMD” row re-ran the full configuration). The exceptions are “w/o future covariates”, which necessarily disables every covariate pathway at once (the full-resolution path of Eq. (5) has no input without them, so its 8.00% subsumes the 5.38% of the covariate-path row rather than adding to it), and “w/ causal error correction”, which adds a stage instead of removing one. The study is run on GEFCom2014, the only benchmark with meteorological covariates. Table 7 lists the variants and Fig. 8 ranks them against the full model. On a temperature-rich series the two covariate mechanisms dominate. Removing the future known covariates raises MAPE from 4.73% to 8.00%, and removing the full-resolution covariate path of Eq. (5) raises it to 5.38% — the same value the model attained before that path was added, and by far the largest single architectural effect. Cross-attention (5.04% when removed) and the per-component linear base (4.94%) contribute further. The decomposition machinery itself is, on this covariate-rich series, close to neutral: removing stage 1 leaves MAPE unchanged (4.73%), and removing patch tokens (4.56%) or RevIN (4.42%) marginally lowers it — a candid indication that the load-signal decomposition is not the source of accuracy when a strong exogenous driver is present. What this does NOT establish is the complementary claim, and we resist the temptation to make it: no ablation was run on PJM or AEMO, so we cannot say the decomposition is load-bearing there. What we can say is that the same architecture leads both univariate benchmarks while its decomposition contributes nothing measurable on the covariate-rich one, which is suggestive but is a ranking argument, not an ablation. The reading we are willing to defend is therefore the narrow one: when a strong exogenous driver is available, direct covariate modelling supersedes decomposition-based inductive biases. Extending the ablation to a univariate benchmark is the single most informative experiment this work leaves undone. Causal error correction degrades accuracy (4.87% against 4.73%), consistent with residuals that are close to white noise once the forecast is unbiased, though with five seeds and spreads of ±0.15 and ±0.11 this particular difference is not resolved; it is disabled in the final framework.', bold=False, italic=False)
figure(FIG_FILES[8])
par('Fig. 8. Ablation on GEFCom2014, mean ± std over five seeds; each row changes exactly one component of the full model, except “w/o future covariates” (which disables every covariate pathway at once, so its effect subsumes the covariate-path row) and “w/ causal error correction” (which adds a stage). Red marks components whose removal degrades accuracy, blue those whose removal improves it and grey those with no measurable effect (tolerance 0.05 pp). The two covariate mechanisms dominate; the decomposition machinery is neutral on this covariate-rich benchmark.', size=9.0, bold=False, italic=False)
