par('5.3. Statistical significance', style='Heading 2')
par('Table 8 reports the Diebold–Mariano statistic of the proposed model against every baseline on each dataset (squared-error loss; negative favours the proposed model), with Holm–Bonferroni-adjusted p-values across each dataset’s family of thirteen comparisons. Statistics are computed on the five-seed mean loss profile at the operational stride, and the absolute-error variant (closer to the MAPE headline) is discussed below. Three facts stand out. First, on no dataset does any baseline significantly outperform the proposed model. On AEMO every DM statistic is negative (proposed better); on PJM ten of thirteen are negative, with TCN (+0.21), DLinear (+0.81) and PatchTST (+0.99) marginally positive and nowhere near significance (p_Holm = 1.00); and on GEFCom2014 ten are positive, but the largest (CNN-LSTM +2.25, TCN +2.18) do not survive the multiplicity correction (p_Holm ≥ 0.256). That statement should be read together with its uncorrected counterpart, so we give it: on GEFCom2014 four baselines — CNN-LSTM (p = 0.026), TCN (0.030), GRU (0.036) and Transformer (0.037) — are ahead of the proposed model at the nominal 5% level, and it is the thirteen-way Holm correction that removes them. The correction is the right test for a family of thirteen comparisons and we apply it symmetrically to our own favourable comparisons, but “no baseline beats it” on GEFCom2014 states what survives multiplicity control at n = 259; it is not evidence of parity. Second, the proposed model significantly beats seasonal persistence on all three datasets and seasonal ARIMA on GEFCom2014 and PJM (p_Holm < 0.001); it also beats LSTM, BiLSTM, GRU, CNN-LSTM and GRU-TCN-Attention on PJM, and LSTM, BiLSTM, CNN-LSTM and GRU-TCN-Attention on AEMO, where GRU is borderline (p_Holm = 0.054). Third, under the absolute-error loss the picture is stronger: the proposed model has a negative DM statistic against every one of the thirteen baselines on both PJM and AEMO, and beats PatchTST, ARIMA and seasonal-naïve on GEFCom2014. The sample sizes (n = 259 / 902 / 274; Table 2) mean the GEFCom2014 and AEMO tests have less power than PJM, so their non-significant results should be read as statistical parity rather than proof of equality. All claims are backed by the test itself — not, as in V3, by the assertion that improvements “would survive any reasonable statistical test”.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('Table 8. Diebold–Mariano statistic (squared-error loss, 5-seed mean loss profile) of the proposed model vs each baseline; negative favours the proposed model. Gc = GEFCom2014. p_Holm is Holm-adjusted within each dataset (13 comparisons). No baseline significantly beats the proposed model on any dataset.', size=10.0, bold=True)
table(['Baseline', 'DM Gc', 'p_Holm Gc', 'DM PJM', 'p_Holm PJM', 'DM AEMO', 'p_Holm AEMO'], [['Seasonal-naïve', '−7.68', '<0.001', '−9.84', '<0.001', '−5.41', '<0.001'], ['ARIMA', '−6.09', '<0.001', '−6.59', '<0.001', '−1.01', '0.94'], ['XGBoost', '+0.67', '0.88', '−1.26', '1.00', '−2.23', '0.13'], ['LightGBM', '+0.77', '0.88', '−0.88', '1.00', '−1.96', '0.20'], ['LSTM', '+1.87', '0.38', '−4.12', '<0.001', '−3.27', '0.013'], ['BiLSTM', '+1.75', '0.38', '−3.39', '0.007', '−2.86', '0.041'], ['GRU', '+2.11', '0.29', '−3.75', '0.002', '−2.73', '0.054'], ['TCN', '+2.18', '0.27', '+0.21', '1.00', '−2.59', '0.070'], ['Transformer', '+2.10', '0.29', '−1.45', '0.89', '−2.59', '0.070'], ['CNN-LSTM', '+2.25', '0.26', '−3.25', '0.010', '−3.69', '0.003'], ['GRU-TCN-Attention', '+1.15', '0.75', '−3.10', '0.014', '−3.14', '0.019'], ['DLinear', '+1.83', '0.38', '+0.81', '1.00', '−0.93', '0.94'], ['PatchTST', '−2.35', '0.22', '+0.99', '1.00', '−0.28', '0.94']], caption='Table 9. Like-for-like ensemble comparison: the five-seed mean-prediction ensemble of EVERY model, ranked by MAPE (%) within each dataset. Reported separately from Tables 4–6 because placing one model’s ensemble in a ranking of single-seed models is not a comparison of equals — the ensemble buys its gain with five times the training budget, which every model here is given. The proposed ensemble is first of twelve on PJM and AEMO and ninth of twelve on GEFCom2014. Seasonal-naïve and ARIMA are deterministic and have no ensemble. No baseline ensemble significantly beats the proposed ensemble on any dataset.')
table(['Rank', 'GEFCom2014', 'MAPE', 'PJM', 'MAPE', 'AEMO', 'MAPE'], [['1', 'TCN', '3.70', 'Proposed', '4.75', 'Proposed', '5.22'], ['2', 'CNN-LSTM', '3.76', 'PatchTST', '4.78', 'DLinear', '5.39'], ['3', 'LSTM', '3.85', 'TCN', '5.05', 'PatchTST', '5.46'], ['4', 'Transformer', '3.88', 'DLinear', '5.06', 'Transformer', '5.97'], ['5', 'GRU', '3.88', 'Transformer', '5.15', 'GRU-TCN-Att.', '5.98'], ['6', 'BiLSTM', '3.95', 'LightGBM', '5.19', 'LightGBM', '6.14'], ['7', 'GRU-TCN-Att.', '4.09', 'CNN-LSTM', '5.19', 'BiLSTM', '6.17'], ['8', 'DLinear', '4.18', 'XGBoost', '5.23', 'LSTM', '6.25'], ['9', 'Proposed', '4.27', 'GRU-TCN-Att.', '5.24', 'GRU', '6.27'], ['10', 'LightGBM', '4.35', 'BiLSTM', '5.27', 'TCN', '6.30'], ['11', 'XGBoost', '4.35', 'GRU', '5.47', 'XGBoost', '6.32'], ['12', 'PatchTST', '5.29', 'LSTM', '5.55', 'CNN-LSTM', '6.59']], caption=None)
par('5.4. Quantifying the decompose-then-split bias', style='Heading 2')
par('Two pieces of evidence isolate the leakage. First, the V3 pipeline itself: under decompose-then-split with stride-1 evaluation, it reported 3.34% MAPE on GEFCom2014 and 1.52% on PJM — apparent improvements of 60% over its own baselines — with additional inconsistencies documented in the code audit (the error-correction stage read test-set ground truth through overlapping windows; its training residuals came from shuffled, cross-component misaligned predictions). Second, a controlled experiment (leakage_demo.py): the same ridge learner over the same splits, fed either components from a global CEEMDAN of the full series (protocol A, invalid) or components computed causally within each window (protocol B, valid). On GEFCom2014, protocol A reports 4.51% MAPE against 8.33% for protocol B — an illusory improvement of 45.9% created purely by the evaluation protocol (Fig. 11), The experiment writes its result to results/leakage_GEFCom2014.json, which is the file this paragraph, Fig. 1 and Fig. 11 are all rendered from. That comparison, however, is not as controlled as it first appears, and the point matters enough to fix rather than to hedge. Protocols A and B share the learner family and the splits but not the component set: A fits one ridge per CEEMDAN component and sums them, B fits a single ridge on the concatenated causal components. Their gap therefore confounds the protocol with the decomposition family and with the fitting scheme, and bounds the leakage effect rather than measuring it. We therefore add a matched contrast in which the decomposition, the feature layout and the learner are identical and only the protocol varies: the same CEEMDAN (20 trials, K = 8) computed over the whole series (A2, 4.69% MAPE) or recomputed inside every input window (C, 8.76%). The isolated protocol effect on GEFCom2014 is 46.4%, within half a percentage point of the unmatched estimate — so the confound was real but immaterial, and the leakage claim survives its own control. Repeating the whole experiment on PJM gives 50.9% (2.91% against 5.92%), so the effect is 46–51% across two benchmarks that differ in region, period and load scale, and it is not specific to the one series on which it was first measured. It is also not an artefact of the error measure: across both benchmarks the same contrast moves MAE and RMSE by 46–56%. Two of the 3,290 windows in arm C on GEFCom2014 failed to converge and fall back to the identity decomposition; the count is recorded in the released result file. One caveat remains: CEEMDAN over a full series is expensive, so each run uses the last 20,000 steps with its own 70/15/15 split, giving 73 and 125 non-overlapping test forecasts rather than the 259 and 902 of the main protocol. It calibrates the size of the bias; it is not a second evaluation of the framework. This mirrors the findings of [7–14] and calibrates how much of the V3 headline gain was an artefact: roughly half of the apparent accuracy of decompose-then-split hybrids is attributable to look-ahead leakage rather than to modelling skill.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('5.5. Computational cost', style='Heading 2')
par('The offline pipeline trained one model per sub-signal: ~30 models totalling 4.66M parameters on GEFCom2014 and 6.37M on PJM, with wall-clock training of 34.7 h and 26.3 h respectively, which is what made multi-seed evaluation impractical (one reason V3 was single-seed). The causal framework trains a single 0.22–0.32M-parameter model per dataset, and the entire five-seed protocol costs 31 min on GEFCom2014 and 2.4 h on PJM — 67× and 11× below a single run of the offline pipeline on the same data. The gap narrows on PJM because its series is the longest of the three, so we report both ends rather than the flattering one; a day-ahead forecast costs 0.45 ms of GPU time amortised over the evaluation batch of 64 (Table 10) — a throughput figure, not a batch-one latency measurement, and labelled as such. Efficiency is reported per forecast with explicit units — the V3 efficiency table mixed per-batch means for baselines with sums of per-batch means across sub-models under a per-sample label.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('Table 10. Cost of the neural models on GEFCom2014, per seed, on a single RTX-class GPU. Inference is wall-clock time per day-ahead forecast amortised over the evaluation batch of 64, i.e. a throughput figure rather than a batch-one latency. The non-neural baselines have no comparable parameter or GPU-time figure and are omitted rather than given a misleading zero.', size=10.0, bold=True)
table(['Model', 'Parameters', 'Train time (s)', 'Inference (ms/forecast, amortised)'], [['Proposed (CPTB)', '254,527', '373', '0.45'], ['PatchTST', '465,880', '124', '0.20'], ['BiLSTM', '610,584', '80', '0.15'], ['LSTM', '258,328', '70', '0.08'], ['GRU', '207,128', '50', '0.05'], ['CNN-LSTM', '122,456', '51', '0.11'], ['TCN', '114,968', '73', '0.23'], ['Transformer', '113,112', '92', '0.15'], ['GRU-TCN-Attention', '101,912', '79', '0.23'], ['DLinear', '26,632', '72', '0.02'], ['Seasonal-naïve / ARIMA / XGBoost / LightGBM', '—', '—', '—']], caption=None)
par('5.6. Interpretability', style='Heading 2')
par('Fig. 9 shows test-set-averaged cross-attention weights for all three datasets. The queries are the seven day-length patches of the INPUT window and the keys are the past-patch and future-patch covariate tokens, so the map reports how the encoder distributes attention between recent history and known-future information — an interpretability claim V3 could not support, since its attention figure was computed on one arbitrary VMD mode of one high-frequency IMF and mislabelled as load–meteorology attention. The measured split is the opposite of what one might expect and is worth stating plainly: on GEFCom2014, 74% of the row-normalised mass sits on the past covariate patches and only 26% on the future ones, the two heaviest columns being the two most recent past days; on PJM and AEMO the balance reverses to 40/60 and 46/54 in favour of the future patches. On the covariate-rich dataset the horizon temperature therefore reaches the forecast mainly through the direct path of Eq. (5) rather than through cross-attention, which is consistent with the ablation: removing that direct path costs 0.65 pp, far more than removing cross-attention (0.31 pp).', bold=False, italic=False)
figure(FIG_FILES[9])
par('Fig. 9. Cross-attention weights averaged over each test set. Queries are the seven day-length patches of the input window; keys are the past covariate patches (P) and the future horizon patches (F, right of the divider). The past/future split of the row-normalised mass is printed above each panel and differs sharply between the covariate-rich and the univariate benchmarks.', size=9.0, bold=False, italic=False)
figure(FIG_FILES[10])
par('Fig. 10. Absolute percentage error across the forecast horizon (median, with interquartile band in the left panel, over every day-ahead forecast in the test period). Left: indexed by lead time, normalised so the hourly and half-hourly benchmarks are comparable. Right: the same medians indexed by wall-clock time. Because one forecast is issued per day over a one-day horizon, the two indexings are equivalent and lead time is confounded with time of day within every dataset; the three benchmarks are issued at different hours (15:00, 08:00 and 23:00, marked), which is what makes the right panel worth plotting. The discontinuity at each issue marker puts the end of one forecast and the start of the next one time step apart on the clock, so it is essentially a lead-time contrast; the AEMO midday hump, absent on the other two benchmarks, is a time-of-day pattern — though those benchmarks differ in region, period and resolution too, so neither contribution is isolated here (Section 5.7(vi)).', size=9.0, bold=False, italic=False)
figure(FIG_FILES[11])
par('Fig. 11. The leakage experiment of Section 5.4, on both benchmarks it has been run on. MAPE is plotted because it is the one metric comparable across series whose loads differ by three orders of magnitude; the MAE and RMSE effects are larger (46-56 % across both). The matched pair (left of each panel) uses the same CEEMDAN, the same feature layout and the same ridge, computed over the whole series or recomputed inside each input window, so the protocol is the only difference: it accounts for 46.4 % on GEFCom2014 and 50.9 % on PJM. The lighter pair is the contrast as first run, which also swaps the decomposition family and the fitting scheme and gives 45.9 %; it bounds the effect rather than isolating it. Generated by leakage_demo.py and figures_results.py from results/leakage_GEFCom2014.json.', size=9.0, bold=False, italic=False)
par('5.7. Threats to validity', style='Heading 2')
par('Six limitations bound what these results support, and we state them rather than leave them to be discovered.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('(i) Statistical power. The Diebold–Mariano tests operate on n = 259, 902 and 274 non-overlapping day-ahead forecasts. With thirteen comparisons per dataset and a Holm correction, only sizeable differences can reach significance, so “no baseline significantly beats the proposed model” must be read as statistical parity under this sample size, not as demonstrated equality. On GEFCom2014 in particular the point estimates do favour several baselines, and a larger test period could well resolve that gap against us. An equivalence test, or a model-confidence-set procedure, would sharpen this and is the natural next step.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('(ii) One chronological split per dataset. The 70/15/15 division follows common practice and keeps the fourteen-model grid affordable, but every conclusion rides on a single test period; PJM alone provides a multi-year one. A rolling-origin evaluation of the leading models would test whether the ranking is stable across origins.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('(iii) The covariate set. Horizon temperature is supplied as a perfect-forecast proxy on GEFCom2014, following the competition’s own convention, so the GEFCom2014 figures are upper bounds with respect to weather-forecast uncertainty; rankings are unaffected because every model receives the identical proxy. All baselines take future covariates through one shared conditioning head rather than natively, so we claim parity of the information set, not of the fusion mechanism; a covariate-native architecture such as TiDE or TFT is the baseline we would add next.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('(iv) The ablation is run on GEFCom2014 only. Its central finding — that the covariate pathways, not the decomposition, carry the accuracy there — is therefore established for the covariate-rich regime; the univariate regime is characterised only indirectly, by the fact that the same architecture leads both univariate benchmarks.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('(v) One design choice was made per dataset, and an earlier version made it badly. Whether the covariate path of Eq. (5) is active is selected by the validation-only rule of Eq. (6); in the previous version of this work the same switch was tied to the presence of temperature, a rule that had in fact been settled on after looking at test scores. We report this because a paper whose contribution is a leakage argument owes the reader its own audit trail, and because it illustrates that leakage enters through author decisions as readily as through preprocessing. No other hyperparameter is tuned per dataset: the architecture, optimiser and schedule of Section 3 are identical everywhere.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('(vi) Lead time and time of day are confounded by the protocol. One forecast per day over a one-day horizon means the k-th step of every forecast falls at the same clock time, so no lead-time curve computed under this protocol can separate the two (Fig. 10). The issue hour itself is an artefact of where the chronological split falls rather than an operational choice, and it differs across the three benchmarks. Nothing in the model comparison depends on this — every model is trained and evaluated on exactly the same issue times — but the per-lead-time diagnostics must be read with it in mind, and a design that varies the issue hour would be needed to attribute error to lead time alone.', bold=False, italic=False)
par('6. Conclusions', style='Heading 1')
par('This paper set out to improve a decomposition-based STLF framework and, in doing so, confronted a defect that affects a large fraction of the published literature: decomposing the full series before the train–test split leaks future information and inflates apparent accuracy. The proposed framework retains the adaptive dual-stage identity — multi-scale separation, band-pass refinement of high-frequency content and complexity-guided routing — but realizes it causally inside the model, where it is learnable, reconstruction-exact and machine-checkably leak-free, and augments it with a full-resolution covariate path so that a strong exogenous driver such as temperature can reach the forecast directly. A single compact network replaces ~30 offline sub-models at a small fraction of the parameter count, making a five-seed, significance-tested evaluation affordable.', bold=False, italic=False)
par("Under this honest protocol the framework attains the lowest MAPE of all fourteen models on both univariate benchmarks (PJM and AEMO — first on every metric on AEMO, fourth on RMSE and R² on PJM), and on the temperature-driven GEFCom2014 it is statistically on par with the strongest baselines. No baseline outperforms it with significance on any dataset, though on GEFCom2014 four of them do so before the multiplicity correction (Section 5.3), so this is parity under a thirteen-way Holm correction and not a demonstrated tie. It beats seasonal persistence on all three benchmarks and seasonal ARIMA on two, the exception being AEMO, where ARIMA's forecasts are so erratic that the test cannot resolve a difference despite a large point-error gap. Equally informative is where the framework does not lead: the ablation shows that on GEFCom2014 the load-signal decomposition is close to neutral and the covariate mechanisms carry the accuracy. Whether the univariate case is its true mirror image we do not know: the ablation was run only on GEFCom2014, so the univariate half of that reading rests on the ranking alone. The honest reading is therefore not that one architecture dominates, but that decomposition-based inductive biases are most valuable for univariate load and yield to direct covariate modelling when a strong exogenous driver is present — a nuance that the leaky protocol of prior work obscured entirely. The controlled leakage experiment shows that the invalid protocol manufactures improvements comparable to those reported by offline hybrids, which we hope encourages routine leakage audits in energy forecasting. Future work includes probabilistic extensions (quantile heads on the same architecture, evaluated with pinball loss on GEFCom2014 [1]), a covariate-native baseline such as TiDE or TFT for the temperature-rich regime, transfer across regions, and replacing the fixed stage-1 kernels with learned multi-scale kernels.", bold=False, italic=False)
